---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';

const guardCode = `# backend/agent.py — inside the add_item tool
draft = ItemDraft(fields=fields, parent_id=parent_id)
outcome = service.preview_item(owner_id, book_id, draft)
if not isinstance(outcome, Ready):
    return outcome.model_dump_json(), outcome   # needs_more / invalid: ask the user

# Valid, but did the user say these numbers? A small model may make them up.
if made_up := ungrounded(fields, _said(state["messages"])):
    ask = NeedsMore(missing=made_up)
    return f"{ask.model_dump_json()}\\nThe user didn't give these values. Ask; never guess.", ask

answer = interrupt({"action": "add_item", "name": item.name, "summary": summary})
if answer != "yes":
    return CANCELLED, outcome
service.commit_item(owner_id, book_id, item, outcome.value.parent_id)`;

const groundingCode = `# backend/grounding.py
def ungrounded(fields: dict[str, Any], sources: Sequence[str]) -> list[str]:
    """The fields whose number or date appears in none of \`sources\`."""
    said = set().union(*(numbers_in(text) for text in sources))   # "$20k" -> 20000
    missing = []
    for path, value in leaves(fields):                              # "interest.rate"
        number = _decimal(value)
        if number is not None and number not in said and number * 100 not in said:
            missing.append(path)                                     # 0.07 is grounded by "7%"
    return missing`;

const evalCode = `# evals/model_eval.py — a task passes when the BOOK is right, not when the reply sounds right
uv run python evals/model_eval.py --runs 3 qwen2.5:3b llama3.2:3b
LLM_PROVIDER=openrouter uv run --env-file .env python evals/model_eval.py`;
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="ChatFinance"
  kicker="Case study · Agentic AI · Python"
  lead="Financial forecasting where you describe your plans in plain English. An AI agent turns the conversation into a typed financial model; tested code does every calculation, and nothing is saved until you say yes."
  meta={[
    { label: 'Role', value: 'Solo: product, design, engineering' },
    { label: 'Stack', value: 'Python · LangGraph · FastAPI · React · Docker' },
    { label: 'Status', value: 'Live demo, Phase 1 complete' },
  ]}
  links={[
    { label: 'Try the live demo ↗', href: 'https://chatfinance.duckdns.org', primary: true },
    { label: 'See the Figma design ↗', href: 'https://www.figma.com/design/nwenl5yhPejlZaykq1MEGv' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="container">
    <Figure
      src="/images/chatfinance/live-charts.jpg"
      alt="ChatFinance: chat on the left, a dashboard with net worth, cash by year and loan balance charts on the right"
      width={1360}
      height={728}
      caption="The live app: a conversation on the left, a 10-year forecast on the right."
    />
  </div>

  <section class="container cs-section">
    <div class="cs-grid">
      <h2>The problem</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          I used to work on budgeting and forecasting software. The calculations were never the hard part:
          a loan, a depreciation schedule or a tax bracket is a handful of well-understood equations, and the
          forecast itself is deterministic, tested code.
        </p>
        <p>
          The hard part was <strong>input</strong>. Every asset, loan, income stream, cost, bank account,
          depreciation rule and tax setting had to be entered through forms with dozens of fields. Users gave
          up long before the model was complete, so the forecast was only ever as good as the patience of
          the person typing.
        </p>
        <p class="pull">
          Large language models are good at exactly the part people find tedious: understanding what someone
          means. They are bad at exactly the part that must be right: arithmetic.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container cs-section">
    <div class="cs-grid">
      <h2>Objectives</h2>
      <ol class="objectives">
        <li>
          <strong>Input by conversation.</strong>
          <span>“Add a car loan of $20,000 at 7% over 5 years” becomes a valid, typed loan item, and the agent asks for whatever is missing.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Numbers come from code, never the model.</strong>
          <span>Repayments, transfer duty and every forecast figure are computed by the tested engine. The LLM only extracts values and asks questions.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A person approves every write.</strong>
          <span>The agent pauses and shows exactly what it is about to save. Nothing reaches the book until the user says yes.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Scenarios from public data.</strong>
          <span>“Can I afford an investment property?” The agent identifies every cost involved (duty, fees, rates, insurance), projects growth from historical averages and forecasts the time to break even, citing each assumption.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Measurable, not vibes.</strong>
          <span>Evals with real pass rates and a trace of every run, so a model or prompt change is a measured decision.</span>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container cs-section">
    <div class="cs-grid">
      <h2>How a request flows</h2>
      <div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          One message, from words to a saved, forecast item. Each step that could go wrong has a check in code
          rather than an instruction in a prompt.
        </p>
        <ol class="flow">
          <li><span class="flow-step">You</span><span>“Add a car loan of $20,000 at 7% over 5 years.”</span></li>
          <li><span class="flow-step">LLM</span><span>Picks the <code>add_item</code> tool and extracts the fields it heard. Tool schemas come straight from the Pydantic models.</span></li>
          <li class="check"><span class="flow-step">Validate</span><span>The service layer returns <code>Ready</code>, <code>NeedsMore</code> or <code>Invalid</code>. A missing start date goes back to the user as a question.</span></li>
          <li class="check"><span class="flow-step">Ground</span><span>Every number and date in the tool call must appear in what the user actually said. Invented values are rejected.</span></li>
          <li class="check"><span class="flow-step">Confirm</span><span>LangGraph <code>interrupt()</code> pauses the graph and the UI shows a Yes/No card with the exact item.</span></li>
          <li><span class="flow-step">Commit</span><span>On yes, the service writes the item; the engine recomputes the forecast.</span></li>
          <li><span class="flow-step">Update</span><span>The chat stream (SSE) emits <code>book_changed</code>; the dashboard refetches and redraws.</span></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container cs-section">
    <div class="cs-grid">
      <h2>Working example</h2>
      <div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          A real session on the live demo. The user leaves out the start date; the agent asks instead of
          guessing, then shows the loan for approval.
        </p>
        <div class="shots-2">
          <Figure
            src="/images/chatfinance/live-confirm.jpg"
            alt="The agent asks for the loan's start date, then shows a confirmation card with principal 20000, 7% compound interest, 5 years, starting 2026-12-01, and Yes / No buttons"
            width={1360}
            height={728}
            label="Human in the loop"
            caption="“needs more” → the agent asks for the date → a confirmation card with every field it will save."
          />
          <Figure
            src="/images/chatfinance/live-reports.jpg"
            alt="The Reports tab showing a quarterly cash report for 2026 to 2032 with totals and closing balances"
            width={1360}
            height={728}
            label="Deterministic output"
            caption="Reports are computed by the forecasting engine: profit, assets, credit, cash, depreciation and interest, monthly to annual."
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container cs-section">
    <div class="cs-grid">
      <h2>Architecture</h2>
      <div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          The service layer is the core: plain Python, Pydantic in and out, no HTTP and no LLM. Every way in is
          a thin adapter over it, so the agent, the REST API and the CLI can never disagree about the rules.
        </p>
        <div class="arch" role="img" aria-label="Architecture: React frontend and CLI and agent tools call the service layer, which uses the corefinance engine and a book repository">
          <div class="arch-row">
            <div class="arch-box">React + TypeScript<small>shadcn/ui · TanStack Query · Recharts</small></div>
            <div class="arch-box">CLI<small>argparse · same service calls</small></div>
            <div class="arch-box">MCP server<small>planned</small></div>
          </div>
          <div class="arch-arrow">↓ REST + Server-Sent Events</div>
          <div class="arch-row">
            <div class="arch-box">FastAPI<small>streaming chat, /resume for Yes/No</small></div>
            <div class="arch-box accent">LangGraph agent<small>tool node · checkpointer · interrupt</small></div>
          </div>
          <div class="arch-arrow">↓ direct calls, no HTTP</div>
          <div class="arch-row">
            <div class="arch-box strong">Service layer<small>draft validation · scenarios · duty tables · grounding</small></div>
          </div>
          <div class="arch-arrow">↓</div>
          <div class="arch-row">
            <div class="arch-box">corefinance engine<small>Pydantic v2 · deterministic forecasts</small></div>
            <div class="arch-box">Book repository<small>JSON today, Postgres next</small></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <ul class="facts">
          <li><strong>Model-agnostic.</strong> Ollama locally; OpenAI, OpenRouter, Gemini or Groq by one environment variable. The graph only sees LangChain's chat-model interface.</li>
          <li><strong>Small models are enough</strong> because validation, grounding and the graph do the hard work.</li>
          <li><strong>Ownership from day one.</strong> Every book carries an <code>owner_id</code>; storage sits behind a repository interface.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container cs-section">
    <div class="cs-grid">
      <h2>Guardrails in code</h2>
      <div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          A prompt can ask a model not to invent numbers. Code can make sure it doesn't. Asked to “add a car
          loan”, one small model filled in $25,000 at 5% over 5 years by itself. This is the check that now
          catches it.
        </p>
        <div class="code">
          <Code code={guardCode} lang="python" theme="github-dark-dimmed" wrap />
        </div>
        <div class="code">
          <Code code={groundingCode} lang="python" theme="github-dark-dimmed" wrap />
        </div>
        <ul class="facts">
          <li><strong>Responsible by design:</strong> the system prompt models scenarios only and never recommends what to buy.</li>
          <li><strong>Money is never a float.</strong> Decimal end to end; JSON carries amounts as strings.</li>
          <li><strong>Assumptions are cited:</strong> each one records its value, source, date and whether the user confirmed it.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container cs-section">
    <div class="cs-grid">
      <h2>Evals and tracing</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Small models answer the same prompt differently from run to run; one run passed 5 of 7 tasks, the
          next 4, with nothing changed. So the eval harness runs the app's real tasks against each model, each
          in a fresh book, and scores the <em>book afterwards</em>: right tool, right fields, asked instead of
          guessing, never wrote before confirmation. Results are pass rates over several runs, not a single
          pass or fail.
        </p>
        <div class="code">
          <Code code={evalCode} lang="bash" theme="github-dark-dimmed" wrap />
        </div>
        <p>
          Every conversation is traced in <strong>Langfuse</strong>: graph nodes, LLM and tool calls, tokens,
          latency, cost and validation failures. A model switch is a config change plus an eval re-run.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container cs-section">
    <div class="cs-grid">
      <h2>From Figma to code</h2>
      <div>
        <p class="section-intro">
          I designed every screen in Figma first, including the states that usually get forgotten (empty,
          loading, validation error, dark mode), then built them in React, TypeScript, Tailwind and shadcn/ui.
        </p>
        <Figure
          src="/images/chatfinance/figma-states.png"
          alt="Figma board with six frames: Items, Reports and Charts on desktop, Items in dark mode, the empty first-run state and the loading state"
          width={907}
          height={418}
          label="Figma"
          caption="Design states: items, reports, charts, dark mode, first run and assistant working."
        />
        <div class="shots-2">
          <Figure
            src="/images/chatfinance/figma-charts.png"
            alt="Figma design of the Charts tab with net worth, cash by year, what you own and loan balance charts"
            width={1391}
            height={868}
            label="Design"
          />
          <Figure
            src="/images/chatfinance/live-charts.jpg"
            alt="The built Charts tab in the live app, matching the design's four charts"
            width={1360}
            height={728}
            label="Built"
          />
        </div>
        <p class="note">Same layout, same components, same four charts. The build ships the dark theme by default; light mode is one click away.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container cs-section">
    <div class="cs-grid">
      <h2>Engineering and delivery</h2>
      <div>
        <div class="stats">
          <div><b>230</b><span>automated tests</span></div>
          <div><b>5</b><span>LLM providers, one interface</span></div>
          <div><b>7</b><span>outcome-scored eval tasks</span></div>
          <div><b>2</b><span>containers behind Caddy</span></div>
        </div>
        <ul class="facts">
          <li><strong>CI on every push:</strong> GitHub Actions runs ruff, formatting, mypy and pytest with branch coverage.</li>
          <li><strong>Containerised:</strong> an API image and a web image (Caddy serving the React build and HTTPS) on a cloud VM.</li>
          <li><strong>Safe deploys:</strong> images are tagged with the commit, the script checks the live site after deploying, and one command rolls back.</li>
          <li><strong>Secrets stay out of code:</strong> provider keys come from the environment; the private engine dependency is fetched over the SSH agent, never baked into an image.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container cs-section">
    <div class="cs-grid">
      <h2>What's next</h2>
      <div>
      <div class="roadmap">
        <p class="eyebrow">Next big step · Forecast from real data</p>
        <h3>Connect Money, so the forecast starts from what actually happened</h3>
        <p>
          Today a plan is only as good as what the user remembers. Next, ChatFinance will call <a href="/projects/money/">Money</a>'s API or MCP server for a snapshot of balances and the actual
          transactions behind them, turn them into items in a new book, derive each item's trend and growth from
          its history, and forecast forward from there.
        </p>
        <div class="arch-row">
          <div class="arch-box">Money<small>balances · actuals</small></div>
          <div class="arch-box">API / MCP<small>snapshot + history</small></div>
          <div class="arch-box">New book<small>actuals become items</small></div>
          <div class="arch-box">Trend &amp; growth<small>derived in code from history</small></div>
          <div class="arch-box accent">Forecast<small>the same tested engine</small></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li><strong>Guided start:</strong> “I want to buy an investment property” → the agent picks the scenario, asks for the location, suggests every value with a median, range and source, and confirms group by group.</li>
        <li><strong>Knowledge base on Postgres + pgvector:</strong> date-versioned reference tables and RAG over domain rules, with recall@k evals.</li>
        <li><strong>MCP server</strong> over the same service layer, then login and multi-user books.</li>
      </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container cs-section cs-end">
    <a class="btn primary" href="https://chatfinance.duckdns.org">Try ChatFinance ↗</a>
    <a class="btn" href="/projects/money/">Next: Money →</a>
  </section>
</ProjectLayout>
